# For Fedora, these are the main commands you'll use:

Update Fedora

`sudo dnf upgrade --refresh`

Install software

`sudo dnf install package-name`

Example:

`sudo dnf install vlc`

Remove software

`sudo dnf remove package-name`

Search for software

`dnf search keyword`

Example:

`dnf search firefox`

See installed packages

`dnf list installed`

Check Fedora version

`cat /etc/fedora-release`

or:

`hostnamectl`

System information

`inxi -F`

(Install it first if needed:)

`sudo dnf install inxi`

Disk information

`lsblk`

Check disk space

`df -h`

Check memory

`free -h`

Check running processes

`top`

or better:

`htop`

Install htop:

`sudo dnf install htop`

Restart / shut down

Restart:

`systemctl reboot`

Shutdown:

`systemctl poweroff`

Enable a service

`sudo systemctl enable --now service-name`

Example (SSH server):

`sudo dnf install openssh-server`
`sudo systemctl enable --now sshd`

Check hardware

lspci

USB devices:

lsusb

Network information

ip a

Wi-Fi status:

nmcli device

Fedora uses:

dnf = package manager (like Ubuntu's apt)

systemctl = services

journalctl = logs


Coming from Ubuntu, the biggest change is:

Ubuntu:

sudo apt update
sudo apt upgrade

Fedora:

sudo dnf upgrade --refresh

The rest of the Linux commands (ls, cd, cp, mv, grep, ssh, etc.) are mostly the same.

----------------------------------------
DNF stands for Dandified YUM.

It's Fedora's package manager—the program you use to install, update, remove, and search for software.

Some common commands:

sudo dnf update – Update your system.

sudo dnf install package_name – Install a package.

sudo dnf remove package_name – Remove a package.

dnf search package_name – Search for a package.

dnf info package_name – Show information about a package.


Think of it this way:

Ubuntu: apt

Fedora: dnf


They serve the same purpose; they just work with different Linux distributions.